Architecture:

            Flask-based API backend

            Kafka for ingestion pipeline

            Spark for vectorization and preprocessing

            OpenSearch for vector search and faceted search

            AWS Bedrock for embeddings

            Azure OpenAI for inference

            Deployment: AWS ECS + CML High Availability cluster

            Monitoring: Datadog, Slack alerts, OpenSearch logs


Architecture Infra Details:

            OpenSearch: 12 nodes, 32 vCPU, 128 GB RAM, 5 TB SSD per node

            Spark Cluster: 10 nodes, 16 vCPU, 64 GB RAM, 2 TB SSD (yarn avoids zk)

            Kafka Cluster: 6 nodes, 16 vCPU, 64 GB RAM, 2 TB SSD, replication factor 3, KRaft so no zoo keeper

            Flask Backend: 4 ECS tasks, 4 vCPU, 16 GB RAM each-

            Bedrock & Azure OpenAI: Managed, autoscaling endpoints




configs
# ====================================================================
# 1. OpenSearch HNSW CONFIGS (for 42M Chunks, 1024-Dim Vectors)
#    - Note: Increasing M to 64 slightly boosts recall for 1024D vectors
#            but increases memory significantly. 
#            A value of 48 offers a good balance.
# ====================================================================
OpenSearch_HNSW_M                  48        # Connectivity: Higher for better recall in high-D space
OpenSearch_HNSW_efConstruction     600       # Indexing Quality: High value ensures a high-quality graph
OpenSearch_HNSW_efSearch           100       # Query Quality: Set to 25 * k (25 * 4) for high recall
OpenSearch_HNSW_k                  4         # Retrieval Count

# ====================================================================
# 2. KAFKA PRODUCER CONFIGS (High Throughput Ingestion)
# ====================================================================
Kafka_Topic_Partitions             50        # Assuming 5 brokers (10 partitions/broker) for parallelism
Kafka_Topic_ReplicationFactor      3         # Standard fault tolerance
Kafka_Producer_batch.size          1048576   # 1 MB batch size for network efficiency
Kafka_Producer_linger.ms           50        # Wait 50ms to accumulate full batch

# ====================================================================
# 3. KAFKA CONSUMER CONFIGS (Spark Side Bulk Fetch)
# ====================================================================
Kafka_Consumer_max.poll.records    10000     # Fetch a large number of records per poll for Spark efficiency
Kafka_Consumer_fetch.min.bytes     2097152   # 2 MB minimum fetch size to reduce network overhead
Kafka_Consumer_isolation.level     read_committed # Ensure only committed messages are read

# ====================================================================
# 4. SPARK DRIVER CONFIGS (Job Coordination)
# ====================================================================
Spark_driver_memory                12G       # Increased size for large job metadata/state
Spark_num_executors                20        # Number of worker nodes to distribute work
Spark_default_parallelism          200       # (20 executors * 5 cores * 2) to saturate cores

# ====================================================================
# 5. SPARK EXECUTOR CONFIGS (1024-Dim Embedding Generation)
#    - Based on 8-Core / 64GB Node: Executor memory is 48GB.
# ====================================================================
Spark_executor_memory              48G       # Max heap for 64GB node (reserves 16GB for OS/overhead)
Spark_executor_cores               5         # Optimal core count for concurrency on 8-core node
Spark_memory.fraction              0.8       # 80% of heap dedicated to Unified Memory
Spark_serializer                   org.apache.spark.serializer.KryoSerializer # Faster serialization for large dataframes


